DistortKit: third-party material and acknowledgements
=====================================================

DistortKit is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or a port of the ISOTROPY Software Suite (Brigham Young
University). "ISODISTORT" is the name of that suite's web tool; DistortKit is an independent, clean-room
implementation of similar analyses and shares none of its source code.

1. ISOTROPY label tables  (distortkit/tables/data_isotropy.txt, distortkit/tables/data_irreps.txt)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
These two files are unmodified copies of data files from the ISOTROPY Software Suite by

    Harold T. Stokes, Dorian M. Hatch and Branton J. Campbell
    Department of Physics and Astronomy, Brigham Young University, Provo, Utah, USA
    https://iso.byu.edu

They are used only to turn the irreps found by DistortKit into the standard labels (X5+, R4+, GM4-, ...): the
isotropy subgroups, irreps and child space groups themselves are computed by DistortKit.

The ISOTROPY user manual (version 9.5, September 2021, first page) states that the software may be distributed
without restriction, provided that research using it which results in publications acknowledges the program with
reference to H. T. Stokes, D. M. Hatch and B. J. Campbell. If you publish results that use the irrep labels of
DistortKit, please cite the suite in the form requested on its web site:

    H. T. Stokes, D. M. Hatch and B. J. Campbell, ISOTROPY Software Suite, iso.byu.edu.

These two files are not covered by the MIT licence of this repository. Everything else works without them (only
the label names are lost); to use your own copy set the ISODATA environment variable to the folder holding them.
Questions about their use: Harold T. Stokes, stokesh@byu.edu.

2. Space-group and Wyckoff tables  (distortkit/tables/space_groups.json, wyckoff.json)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Generated by tools/build_tables.py. The 230 space groups (International Tables default settings) are taken from
the database of spglib (BSD-3-Clause; A. Togo, K. Shinohara, I. Tanaka, "Spglib: a software library for crystal
symmetry search", arXiv:1808.01590, https://spglib.readthedocs.io), which in turn credits Yusuke Seto's
crystallographic database. The Wyckoff positions are computed by that script from the space-group operations; the
Wyckoff letters are those of the International Tables for Crystallography, Vol. A, as returned by spglib. The tables
contain only crystallographic facts (group operations and special positions). tools/verify_tables.py checks them
against the ISOTROPY data tables: all 230 groups and all 1731 Wyckoff positions agree once ISOTROPY's different
conventions (primitive-cell basis for centred groups, monoclinic axis, origin choice) are converted.

3. Dependencies (installed by pip, not distributed with DistortKit)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
numpy (BSD-3-Clause); pymatgen (MIT) for symmetrising a structure without symmetry information; matplotlib
(PSF-based licence) and Pillow (HPND) for animated GIFs; spglib (BSD-3-Clause) for the tables and, through pymatgen,
for symmetry detection.
